tested.summary:
WHAT I DID: I created the work folder (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Music/02fc5154-76f5-5aac-a505-860164e7e921/scratchpad/script-rewrite/work-test-fresh-newcomers, left empty; findings are returned here). I read the narration once, page by page, at about 140 words a minute, and answered the five comprehension probes from the toolkit's pinker-teach-for-understanding/SKILL.md (reconstruction, terms, hesitation, examples, application). I opened the ledger only to check that each fix I suggest is supported. I did not open the old script or the drafts. IMPORTANT: these three listeners are my simulation, not real people. The script is still untested with real newcomers.

OVERALL: the two-factories picture works. All three listeners can say the core rule back, and they answer the hospitals case correctly. The guesses land, and "prices go up more slowly. Not down" comes through. The failures are in the links around the rule:
- the closing "Spent on what?" is never taught;
- New Zealand's price outcome is never said;
- the Reserve Bank is never introduced;
- page 2 packs the hardest idea into 18 seconds;
- Mattei's view is missing the step that connects it to prices;
- "a rise in costs" uses a word the script has already used for shop prices.

STUDENT, 17
- Main claim: "Making new money doesn't always push prices up. If people are out of work and machines are idle, it gets more stuff made. If everyone's flat out, prices just go up. When prices go up too fast, the Reserve Bank puts up interest rates and people lose jobs."
- Terms: inflation, the quiet and flat-out factories, spare and interest are all fine. The Reserve Bank is only a name; they assume it is the government. "Economists who study how modern money works" sounds like "the experts". "Spent on what" means "what they buy, like hospitals or a war". "A rise in costs" just means "prices went up".
- Hesitations:
  - page 2: "then why can't the government pay for everything?";
  - pages 10-11: "did prices go up here? it never says";
  - page 12: "is 170,000 a lot?";
  - page 13: "how does weaker pay slow prices?".
- Examples: Britain and Japan are clear. For Glasgow: "where's the new money?" New Zealand is clear, but they assume prices went up.
- New case (hospitals while builders are flat out): prices go up. Correct.
- Drought with no new money: unsure whether it is the flat-out factory (like the sunk ships) or a rise in costs.

RETIRED BUILDER, 60
- Main claim: "If there's slack, new money gets work done. If everyone's flat out, you just push the price up. Then the Reserve Bank jacks up the rates and blokes lose their jobs."
- Terms: strong on flat out, interest and the Reserve Bank. He doesn't trust "economists who study how modern money works" ("which ones?"), and "can't run out of its own money" makes him push back.
- Hesitations:
  - page 2: he stops believing it for a moment;
  - page 9: "why make it if it just sits in the bank?";
  - page 12: "170,000 out of how many?";
  - page 14: he loses "two point nine".
- Examples: Glasgow is his best page. For New Zealand he remembers the 2021-22 building boom and the materials shortage, but the script never says what the money went on, so he can't link the two.
- New case: "prices up, and you'd pinch builders off houses." Strong.

ADULT WHOSE FIRST LANGUAGE ISN'T ENGLISH
- Main claim: "New money not always makes prices up. If workers have no job, money makes more things. If all are busy, prices go up. Then the bank makes loans expensive and people lose jobs."
- Terms:
  - "flat-out" is at risk ("flat" can mean unchanging, or a rented home), though the meaning given just before it rescues it;
  - "what it makes" sounds like the money makes things;
  - "who pays to stop it" is heard literally.
- Hesitations, by page:
  - 2: "What it can run out of...";
  - 3-4: "spent on what it makes";
  - 5: "When there aren't...";
  - 7: "Homes worked", and "by up to a quarter";
  - 11: needs to hear the whole chain again;
  - 13: "Banks like our Reserve Bank" (heard first as "banks like it");
  - 14: "Without... two point nine";
  - 15: "who pays to stop it".
- Examples: Britain, Japan and New Zealand are clear. For Glasgow they get the rent, but not the link to new money.
- New case: correct, unless "flat" was heard as "no change", in which case they answer "prices stay the same".

WHERE THE LISTENERS NEED TO HEAR IT AGAIN: pages 2, 11 and 13 for everyone; also pages 3-4, 7 and 14 for the listener whose first language isn't English.
